━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
+ Instance is Struct

` `fills-share` says a constructor's properties, as it gives them their first values, read a
` lazy local nested functions capture or make a nested function, where TypeScript keeps two
` of each lazy local: one read then and one the body reads. A rule a pattern calls under a
` helper is typed again as an instance of its own, given the `helper` it runs under, which
` `uses-helper` where its pattern makes a value through the helper or calls a rule that needs
` it to roll back, and `rolls-helper` where it needs the helper to roll back. A rule called for
` the text it matched under a helper that observes literal words is typed for the helper as one
` of its own that `spans`, which makes nothing through the helper. A rule a pattern on a String
` calls is typed again as one of its own that is `encoded`, whose buffer holds the String's
` UTF-8 bytes rather than its characters. A rule whose
` pattern holds a rollback mark `carries-mark`, which it gives the pattern calling it. A
` constructor a markup element calls with attributes is an instance of its own: it takes,
` after its arguments, the value of each property in `fills`, and gives the property that
` value in place of the one its class writes.
` A constructor a markup element calls giving slots content is one too: it takes, after
` those, the function that builds each slot in `slots`, which it gives the function that fills
` the object with its class's markup, which is one of its own for those slots too. So is one a
` markup element calls for a class whose markup is its content, for the styles and style
` sheets the element sees, its `context` among `contexts`, and the function that fills it;
` both take, after the values of properties and before the builders of slots, the `carried`
` state cells the context gives (`context-values`). The values a rule applied on a root `<This`
` is given are typed by an instance of their own, which types only the application
` `root-values` (`intern-root-values`). An instance that `casts-errors` lets out an error of a
` class above one it states, cast to that one. A style rule a style sheet writes is typed for
` the class of the `element` it is applied to, whose styles answer its names with the sheet's.
` A function typed in a GC-free zone names the `zone` it was first called in, as TypeScript
` keeps where the zone is entered as it compiles the functions called there. The default of a
` parameter written as a constant is typed with its function, and any other where a call first
` takes it; `typed-defaults` keeps a bit for each of the first 64 typed (`type-default`).
` A method that yields and states no errors, which a class's table holds in the place of a base's
` method whose generator carries errors, is typed again as an instance of its own that states
` those errors, as the reference's method inherits them: it `inherits` them from the base's
` instance, and the instance typed again names it as `retyped`. A constructor of a class above
` the one it makes, called with no value for a parameter named as a property the class it makes
` writes a default of its own for, is one of its own too: `redefaults` keeps a bit for each such
` parameter, among the first 32, whose property it leaves to that default, as the reference's
` `redefaultedArgNames` does. One typed on trial keeps the `probe` that numbers it. One whose
` typing changed what another type pass cannot take in is `unshared` (`Checker.run`), and
` `unretirable` where it changed more than rows retiring it removes (`Facts.retire`) and what an
` edit typed again in place keeps (`Facts.kept-weight`). One whose typing another pass published,
` which this pass took in in place of typing its body, is `imported` (`import-result`): its body
` stays the other's. One written in another whose typing that one's depends on, by what it notes
` there or keeps of it, is typed `with-enclosing`: no pass takes it in from another, and the merge
` takes it from a pass only with the one it is written in (`merge-sessions`).

